@page "/in-dialog"

<PageTitle>Grid in a Dialog — NxGrid</PageTitle>

<h1>Grid in a Dialog</h1>
<p class="doc-intro">
    NxGrid renders its popups — column menu, context menu, combo dropdown, date and color pickers,
    tooltips, and the drag-fill handle — as <code>position:fixed</code> elements so they can escape
    the grid's own scroll container. Fixed coordinates are viewport-relative only while the viewport
    is the containing block. A modal dialog usually breaks that assumption: a <code>transform</code>
    used for centering or an open animation, a <code>filter</code>, a <code>backdrop-filter</code>,
    or CSS <code>contain</code> on any ancestor makes <em>that element</em> the containing block for
    fixed descendants — and a dialog that hides overflow clips them as well. The grid detects this,
    offsets its popups accordingly, and renders them in the browser's top layer so they extend past
    the dialog edge, bounded only by the browser window. No host-side workaround is needed.
</p>

<div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Transformed dialog host</h2>
    <p class="doc-note">
        The dialog below is centered with <code>transform: translate(-50%, -50%)</code> and hides
        overflow — the exact pattern that would otherwise push every popup down and to the right by
        the dialog's own offset and then clip it. Open the column menu, right-click a cell, edit the
        Department column (combo dropdown), hover a cell for its tooltip, and drag the fill handle.
        Everything lines up with its cell, and the taller popups spill outside the dialog rather than
        being cut off at its edge.
    </p>
    <p class="doc-note">
        The Department dropdown sets <code>ComboBoxVirtualizeThreshold="0"</code> so it virtualizes
        even with five options. That makes it re-render once immediately after opening, to pin its
        rows to the row height it just measured — the case that matters here, because a popup keeps
        its position and its theme colors across every re-render, not just from the second open on.
    </p>
    <button class="doc-btn" @onclick="@(() => dialogOpen = true)">Open dialog</button>
    <pre class="doc-code" style="margin-top:12px">@dialogCode</pre>
</div>

<div class="doc-section">
    <h2>Inline transformed container</h2>
    <p class="doc-note">
        The same rule applies without a dialog. This grid sits in a container with
        <code>transform: scale(1)</code> and <code>filter: saturate(1)</code> — both are visual no-ops
        that still establish a containing block. Popups stay anchored to their cell.
    </p>
    <div style="transform:scale(1);filter:saturate(1);border:1px dashed var(--nx-grid-border);padding:12px">
        <NxGrid T="Person" Data="@inlinePeople" OnUpdate="@HandleUpdate"
                Cursor="NxGridCursor.Cell" RowGutter="NxGridRowGutter.Numbers"
                Editable="true" CellTooltip="@GetTooltip">
            <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Id)" Width="50" MaxWidth="50" Editable="false" />
            <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.FirstName)" Width="130" />
            <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.LastName)" Width="130" />
            <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Department)"
                          ComboBoxSource="@(NxGridComboSource.FixedList("Engineering", "Finance", "HR", "Marketing", "Sales"))" />
        </NxGrid>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="doc-section">
    <h2>What creates a containing block</h2>
    <p class="doc-note">
        Any of these on an ancestor of the grid retargets <code>position:fixed</code> descendants
        from the viewport to that element. The grid walks its ancestor chain, finds the first one,
        and subtracts its offset from every popup coordinate:
    </p>
    <table class="doc-table">
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Property</th><th>Typical source</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>transform</code>, <code>translate</code>, <code>rotate</code>, <code>scale</code></td><td>Dialog centering, open/close animations</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>perspective</code></td><td>3D card effects</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>filter</code>, <code>backdrop-filter</code></td><td>Frosted-glass overlays, dimmed backdrops</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>will-change</code></td><td>Animation hints naming any of the above</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>contain: paint / layout / strict / content</code></td><td>Performance containment on a panel</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>container-type</code></td><td>Container queries</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>content-visibility: auto</code></td><td>Deferred rendering of off-screen sections</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="doc-note" style="margin-top:10px">
        When that ancestor also clips overflow (<code>overflow</code> other than <code>visible</code>,
        as most dialogs do), it clips fixed descendants too. Rather than confining popups to the
        dialog, the grid promotes each one to the browser's top layer while it is open
        (<code>popover</code> + <code>showPopover()</code>) — it keeps its place in the DOM, so
        dismissal and theming are unaffected, but nothing clips it and the browser window becomes the
        only boundary. The browser's own <code>[popover]</code> defaults, which would otherwise
        re-centre the popup and paint its text in the UA's color, are overridden for every popup
        whether it is promoted or not. Browsers without Popover API support fall back to flipping and
        clamping popups inside the dialog so they stay fully visible.
    </p>
</div>

@if (dialogOpen)
{
    <div class="demo-dialog-backdrop" @onclick="@(() => dialogOpen = false)"></div>
    <div class="demo-dialog" @onclick:stopPropagation>
        <div class="demo-dialog-title">
            <span>Purchase Order Lines</span>
            <button class="demo-dialog-close" @onclick="@(() => dialogOpen = false)">✕</button>
        </div>
        <div class="demo-dialog-body">
            <NxGrid T="Person" Data="@dialogPeople" OnUpdate="@HandleUpdate"
                    Cursor="NxGridCursor.Cell" RowGutter="NxGridRowGutter.Numbers"
                    Editable="true" CellTooltip="@GetTooltip">
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Id)" Width="50" MaxWidth="50" Editable="false" />
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.FirstName)" Width="130" />
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.LastName)" Width="130" />
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Age)" Width="70" Alignment="NxGridColumnAlignment.Right" />
                @* ComboBoxVirtualizeThreshold="0" forces the virtualized dropdown even for five
                   options, so this dialog exercises the popup that re-renders immediately after
                   its first open — see the note above the dialog. *@
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Department)" ComboBoxVirtualizeThreshold="0"
                              ComboBoxSource="@(NxGridComboSource.FixedList("Engineering", "Finance", "HR", "Marketing", "Sales"))" />
            </NxGrid>
        </div>
    </div>
}

<style>
    .demo-dialog-backdrop {
        position: fixed;
        inset: 0;
        background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.45);
        z-index: 2000;
    }

    /* The centering transform is what makes this dialog the containing block
       for the grid's position:fixed popups. */
    .demo-dialog {
        position: fixed;
        top: 50%;
        left: 50%;
        transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
        width: min(760px, 92vw);
        max-height: 80vh;
        overflow: hidden;
        display: flex;
        flex-direction: column;
        background: var(--nx-grid-surface);
        border: 1px solid var(--nx-grid-border);
        border-radius: 6px;
        box-shadow: 0 12px 48px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.4);
        z-index: 2001;
    }

    .demo-dialog-title {
        display: flex;
        align-items: center;
        justify-content: space-between;
        padding: 10px 14px;
        border-bottom: 1px solid var(--nx-grid-border);
        font-weight: 600;
    }

    .demo-dialog-close {
        background: none;
        border: none;
        cursor: pointer;
        font-size: 14px;
        color: inherit;
    }

    .demo-dialog-body {
        padding: 14px;
        overflow: auto;
    }
</style>

@code {
    bool dialogOpen;

    List<Person> dialogPeople = SampleData.GetPeopleCopy();
    List<Person> inlinePeople = SampleData.GetPeopleCopy();

    void HandleUpdate(NxGridUpdateArgs<Person> args)
    {
        foreach (var rowChange in args.Rows)
            foreach (var change in rowChange.Changes)
                change.Apply(rowChange.Row);
    }

    async Task<object?> GetTooltip(Person row, NxGridColumn<Person> col)
        => await Task.FromResult<object?>($"{row.FirstName} {row.LastName} — {col.EffectiveTitle}");

    const string dialogCode = """
        <!-- Host dialog: the centering transform makes this element the containing
             block for any position:fixed descendant, including NxGrid's popups. -->
        <div class="my-dialog">
            <NxGrid T="Person" Data="@people" Editable="true" OnUpdate="@HandleUpdate">
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.FirstName)" />
                <NxGridColumn Property="@(x => x.Department)"
                              ComboBoxSource="@(NxGridComboSource.FixedList("Engineering", "Finance"))" />
            </NxGrid>
        </div>

        <style>
            .my-dialog {
                position: fixed;
                top: 50%;
                left: 50%;
                transform: translate(-50%, -50%);  /* nothing else required */
                overflow: hidden;
            }
        </style>
        """;
}
